{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791822301,"nonce":"MIRZIR8Dtv7LsLnB1VW1Ht/wF1A=","title":"Avoid boxing generic interface scalar returns in hot signal reads","message":"ChimpMonk's editor selection cache exposed allocations in ISignal\u003cInt\u003e.get(): its generic interface slot returns Dynamic while SignalI32.get returns Int. Concrete calls allocate zero. The compatible-slot dispatch improvement intentionally keeps the boxed ABI for incompatible signatures; this is a further optimization opportunity, not a regression in compatible dispatch.\n\nReproducer in sibling ChimpMonk: repro/ashui_signal_reads/Repro.hx. From ChimpMonk root:\n  haxe -cp repro/ashui_signal_reads -cp example/src -cp ../ashui/haxe -hl editor/bin/signal-read-repro.hl -main Repro\n  ../ash/target/release/ash --preset application editor/bin/signal-read-repro.hl\nStage AshUI's existing blinc_abi library in editor/bin as scripts/editor.sh does.\n\nWarmed 8,192 calls on this Mac: genericInt=131072 bytes (16 bytes/call), concreteInt=0. Both read the exact integer 7 through the same native primitive. Haxe-backed string interface reads allocate zero, isolating scalar return boxing from pointer returns. The editor uses a concrete SignalI32 revision in its render cache; the general generic interface remains useful elsewhere.\n\nInvestigate a guarded adapter or AIR specialization that unboxes primitive returns without making the box escape. Preserve incompatible signatures, dynamic receivers, exceptions and hot reload. Add tests for unchanged allocation and exact returned values across Cranelift, LLVM and hybrid.","files":null}]}